[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say
otherwise. They describe more than one problem, and the queued parser fix closes only one of them.
Each problem now has its own ticket, with its own count.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close the other tickets. Keep #4471 open until each ticket above is fixed.
</Callout>

## How the reports split

Placed by tenant and channel with `causes.ts`, not by subject and not by intake tag.

- **Shared parser.** The parser reads every date month first, and every tenant here writes the
  day first. 7 April comes out as 4 July. This is what #4471 says it is.
- **booking-v3 form hint.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People
  type month first, as told, and the form reads the value the tenant's way, day first. It is the
  same swap with a different cause. Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants land here. The same
  tenants' app and email reports belong to the parser.
- **One-day move.** In these reports day and month do not swap. The date moves by one day: one
  meeting held on Monday in Toronto and Tuesday in Lisbon, an invite that arrived before its
  booking. The desk noted them as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and still filed them under
  the parser by tenant. A parser fix leaves them as they are. They point to a time zone or
  daylight saving conversion.

## Why one ticket hid them

- Subject lines do not separate causes. Most subjects in #4471 appear under more than one
  problem, so merging on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) puts
  them all in one ticket.
- The intake tag guesses from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the tenant. The
  *intake tagged as* column above shows how often the two disagree.
- The ticket-system export repeats rows across pages. Count by report id.

